ADC / ONBOARD TEMPERATURE: RAW VS DECODED VALUES

A safe first MCP example is the direct get_temperature() tool.
  get_temperature()
This is a decoded numerical temperature from the onboard sensor.
The die temperature is not necessarily ambient air temperature.

Device path:
  /dev/adc/temp
Do not assume the raw device returns ASCII decimal degrees.
Inspect the matching implementation or documented encoding before
interpreting a raw read. A device may not support arbitrary seek.

CHECK
  pins
  ls /dev/adc
  get_temperature()   (MCP)

TROUBLESHOOTING
  The temperature may vary with CPU load and board enclosure.
  A rapid oscillation in the value is not proof of an ADC fault.
  Missing /dev/adc entries may reflect board/firmware configuration.
  External ADC GPIO inputs must respect electrical limits; no
  speculative voltage measurements through unknown wiring.

Agent reporting: state whether a value came from the MCP tool or
from raw bytes, the unit if known, and any conversion uncertainty.
